/-
# Just infinite groups of intermediate growth and branch groups (Kourovka Notebook Problem 15.17)

Kourovka Notebook, Archive of Solved Problems, Problem 15.17 (L. Bartholdi, R. I. Grigorchuk, Z.
Šunić): an infinite group is just infinite if all of its proper quotients are finite. Is every
finitely generated just infinite group of intermediate growth necessarily a branch group?
-/

module

public import Batteries.Util.ProofWanted
public import Mathlib.Analysis.SpecialFunctions.Pow.Real
public import Mathlib.GroupTheory.Finiteness
public import MathlibExt.GroupTheory.GroupGrowth

@[expose] public section

namespace MathlibExt.GroupTheory.JustInfiniteIntermediateGrowthBranchKourovka15Problem17Wanted

/-!
Source: E. I. Khukhro and V. D. Mazurov (eds.), Unsolved Problems in Group Theory: The Kourovka
Notebook, 21st issue, arXiv:1401.0300v49 (2026), Archive of Solved Problems, Problem 15.17 (L.
Bartholdi, R. I. Grigorchuk, Z. Šunić). UnsolvedMath record UM-00701105 (`KOU-15.17`), dataset
ulamai/UnsolvedMath v1.8.0.

Branch groups follow Problem 15.12: a faithful, level-transitive action by automorphisms on a rooted
tree in which every rigid level stabilizer (the subgroup generated by the rigid vertex stabilizers
of the vertices of one level, a rigid vertex stabilizer consisting of the elements whose support
lies in the subtree below the vertex) has finite index. Level-transitivity forces the tree to be
spherically homogeneous, so the tree is modelled as the words whose `i`-th letter is below `m i`,
with all branching indices `m i ≥ 2` as in Grigorchuk's setting (the survey cited in 15.12); tree
automorphisms are the permutations of the vertices preserving the prefix (ancestor) order in both
directions. This is the geometric definition of L. Bartholdi, R. I. Grigorchuk and Z. Šunić, Branch
groups, Handbook of Algebra 3 (2003), arXiv:math/0510294 (Definition 1.13, on the trees of words
with all `m i ≥ 2` of Section 1.2), which their open-problems chapter uses for its versions of
Problems 15.14 (b), (c), 15.15, 15.17 and 15.18 (Questions 20, 15, 14, 10, 9 and 11); Kionke and
Schesler (Section 2.1) and Bondarenko (Section 2) use the same definition. Growth rates follow
Problem 14.7: `ω(G, P) = limsup β_P(n)^(1/n)`, where `β_P(n)` counts the elements of word length at
most `n` for a finite generating family `P`; the growth type does not depend on the generating set,
and the definitions below quantify over every finite generating family. Intermediate growth means
growth rate `1` (subexponential) while the growth function is not polynomially bounded (MathlibExt's
`HasPolynomialGrowth` fails).
-/

/-- **Vertices of a spherically homogeneous rooted tree.** For a sequence $m : \mathbb{N} \to
\mathbb{N}$ of branching indices, the vertices of the spherically homogeneous rooted tree with
branching sequence $m$ are the finite words $x_0 x_1 \cdots x_{\ell-1}$ of natural numbers with
$x_i < m(i)$ for every $i < \ell$. The length of a word is its level and its prefixes are its
ancestors. -/
def TreeVertex (m : ℕ → ℕ) : Type :=
  {l : List ℕ // ∀ (i : ℕ) (h : i < l.length), l[i] < m i}

/-- **Rigid vertex stabilizer.** Let $\rho$ be a homomorphism from a group $G$ to the group of
permutations of the vertices of the rooted tree with branching sequence $m$, and let $v$ be a
vertex. The rigid vertex stabilizer of $v$ is the set of elements $g \in G$ such that $\rho(g)$
fixes every vertex that does not have $v$ as a prefix, that is, the elements whose support (the set
of vertices they move) lies in the subtree rooted at $v$. -/
def rigidVertexStabilizer {m : ℕ → ℕ} {G : Type*} [Group G] (ρ : G →* Equiv.Perm (TreeVertex m))
    (v : TreeVertex m) : Set G :=
  {g | ∀ w : TreeVertex m, ¬ v.1 <+: w.1 → ρ g w = w}

/-- **Rigid level stabilizer.** Let $\rho$ be a homomorphism from a group $G$ to the group of
permutations of the vertices of the rooted tree with branching sequence $m$. For a natural number
$n$, the $n$-th rigid level stabilizer is the subgroup of $G$ generated by the union of the rigid
vertex stabilizers of all vertices at level $n$, that is, of all words of length $n$. -/
def rigidLevelStabilizer {m : ℕ → ℕ} {G : Type*} [Group G] (ρ : G →* Equiv.Perm (TreeVertex m))
    (n : ℕ) : Subgroup G :=
  Subgroup.closure (⋃ v ∈ {v : TreeVertex m | v.1.length = n}, rigidVertexStabilizer ρ v)

/-- **Branch groups.** A group $G$ is a branch group (Kourovka 15.12) if there are a sequence
$m : \mathbb{N} \to \mathbb{N}$ with $m(i) \ge 2$ for all $i$ and a homomorphism $\rho$ from $G$ to
the group of permutations of the vertices of the rooted tree with branching sequence $m$ such that
$\rho$ is injective; every $\rho(g)$ is a tree automorphism, that is, for all vertices $v$ and $w$,
$v$ is a prefix of $w$ if and only if $\rho(g)(v)$ is a prefix of $\rho(g)(w)$; the action is
level-transitive, that is, for any two vertices $v$ and $w$ of the same level there is $g \in G$
with $\rho(g)(v) = w$; and for every $n$ the $n$-th rigid level stabilizer has finite index in
$G$. -/
def IsBranchGroup (G : Type*) [Group G] : Prop :=
  ∃ (m : ℕ → ℕ) (_ : ∀ i, 2 ≤ m i) (ρ : G →* Equiv.Perm (TreeVertex m)),
    Function.Injective ρ ∧
    (∀ (g : G) (v w : TreeVertex m), v.1 <+: w.1 ↔ (ρ g v).1 <+: (ρ g w).1) ∧
    (∀ v w : TreeVertex m, v.1.length = w.1.length → ∃ g : G, ρ g v = w) ∧
    ∀ n : ℕ, (rigidLevelStabilizer ρ n).FiniteIndex

/-- **Growth rate of a finite generating family.** Let $P$ be a generating family of a group $G$
indexed by $\{0, \dots, k-1\}$, and let $\beta_P(n)$ be the number of elements of $G$ whose word
length with respect to $P$ is at most $n$, that is, which are products of at most $n$ members of
$P$ and their inverses. The growth rate of $P$ is the real number
$\omega(G, P) = \limsup_{n \to \infty} \beta_P(n)^{1/n}$. -/
noncomputable def growthRate {G : Type*} [Group G] {k : ℕ} (P : Group.Generators G (Fin k)) : ℝ :=
  Filter.limsup (fun n : ℕ => (Nat.card {g : G // P.wordLength g ≤ n} : ℝ) ^ ((1 : ℝ) / n))
    Filter.atTop

/-- **Just infinite groups.** A group $G$ is just infinite if it is infinite and every nontrivial
normal subgroup of $G$ has finite index, that is, all proper quotients of $G$ are finite. -/
def IsJustInfinite (G : Type*) [Group G] : Prop :=
  Infinite G ∧ ∀ N : Subgroup G, N.Normal → N ≠ ⊥ → N.FiniteIndex

/-- **Groups of intermediate growth.** A group $G$ has intermediate growth if it is finitely
generated and, for every natural number $k$ and every generating family $P$ of $G$ indexed by
$\{0, \dots, k-1\}$, the growth rate of $P$ is at most $1$ (subexponential growth) and $P$ does
not have polynomial growth. -/
def HasIntermediateGrowth (G : Type*) [Group G] : Prop :=
  Group.FG G ∧ ∀ (k : ℕ) (P : Group.Generators G (Fin k)),
    growthRate P ≤ 1 ∧ ¬ P.HasPolynomialGrowth

/-- **The branch question for just infinite groups of intermediate growth.** Kourovka
Problem 15.17 (L. Bartholdi, R. I. Grigorchuk, Z. Šunić) is the assertion that every finitely
generated just infinite group of intermediate growth is a branch group. -/
def justInfiniteIntermediateGrowthIsBranch : Prop :=
  ∀ (G : Type) [Group G], Group.FG G → IsJustInfinite G → HasIntermediateGrowth G →
    IsBranchGroup G

/--
**Just infinite groups of intermediate growth need not be branch groups.** It is not the case
that every finitely generated just infinite group of intermediate growth is a branch group: the
Kourovka Archive answers Problem 15.17 "No, not every".

Nekrashevych constructed infinite finitely generated simple groups of intermediate growth. Such a
group is just infinite, and it is not a branch group: its rigid level stabilizers, nontrivial normal
subgroups of finite index, would be the whole group, which they split as a direct product of at
least two nontrivial factors.

Source: E. I. Khukhro and V. D. Mazurov (eds.), Unsolved Problems in Group Theory: The Kourovka
Notebook, 21st issue, arXiv:1401.0300v49 (2026), Archive of Solved Problems, Problem 15.17 (L.
Bartholdi, R. I. Grigorchuk, Z. Šunić); resolution: V. Nekrashevych, Palindromic subshifts and
simple periodic groups of intermediate growth, Ann. of Math. (2) 187 (2018), no. 3, 667-719,
abstract (infinite finitely generated periodic groups of intermediate growth, among them the first
simple groups of intermediate growth); Kourovka Archive, Problem 15.17 (answer: No, not every; there
exist simple finitely generated groups of intermediate growth; the Archive prints the volume as 87),
https://arxiv.org/abs/1601.01033.
-/
public theorem_wanted justInfiniteIntermediateGrowthIsBranch_refuted :
    ¬ justInfiniteIntermediateGrowthIsBranch

end MathlibExt.GroupTheory.JustInfiniteIntermediateGrowthBranchKourovka15Problem17Wanted
